<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Yale J Biol Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Yale Journal of Biology and Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0044-0086</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1551-4056</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">906554</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2595516</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">906554</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Articles</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Hypertension in Pregnancy: The Use of Ultrasound <xref ref-type="fn" rid="fn1">1</xref></article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hobbins</surname><given-names>John C.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Berkowitz</surname><given-names>Richard L.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><author-notes><fn id="fn1"><label>1</label><p>Presented at the National Meeting on Diagnostic Ultrasound which was held at the Yale University School of Medicine on July 26, 1976. Kenneth J.W. Taylor, M.D., is guest editor.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Jul-Aug</season><year>1977</year></pub-date><volume>50</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>327</fpage><lpage>333</lpage><abstract><p>Great advances in ultrasound instrumentation have enabled the physician to delineate subtle intrauterine changes. Not only can specific measurements of the fetal body be obtained but tissue textures within fetal organs can be appreciated. The perinatologist is constantly searching for ways to learn more about the fetus, and ultrasound has clearly become a major implement in this search. The following discussion will focus on information made available by ultrasound for aid in the modern management of hypertension, one of the most challenging conditions complicating pregnancy.</p><sec sec-type="scanned-figures"><title>Images</title><fig id="F1"><label>FIG. 3</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0012-a" xlink:role="331"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>FIG. 4</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0012-b" xlink:role="331"/></fig></sec></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0008.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="327" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0009.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="328" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0010.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="329" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0011.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="330" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0012.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="331" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0013.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="332" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00139-0014.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="333" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>